@page "/docs/registry/trust"
<PageTitle>Registry trust - Blaizio</PageTitle>

<div class="space-y-10">
    <header class="docs-page-header space-y-2.5">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
            <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Trust</h1>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Secondary">Registry</BzBadge>
        </div>
        <p class="max-w-3xl text-muted-foreground">Installing from a registry copies source code into your project and installs the NuGet packages the item declares. That code compiles and runs as part of your app. This page is what the CLI does about that, and what you can check before saying yes.</p>
    </header>

    <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Where you are asked</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 ps-6 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
            <li><b class="text-foreground">Recording a registry.</b> <CmdChip Command="blaizio registry add" /> says what installing from it will mean and confirms before writing the mapping. <code>-y</code> skips the prompt for scripts.</li>
            <li><b class="text-foreground">A direct URL from an unknown host.</b> <CmdChip Command="blaizio add" /> with a URL whose origin is neither your default registry, nor one you have recorded, nor a listed trusted host, warns and asks the same way.</li>
            <li><b class="text-foreground">A repository address.</b> <code>owner/repo/item</code> asks per REPOSITORY, so approving one project on a host approves that project and nothing else.</li>
            <li><b class="text-foreground">Nothing else.</b> Names, namespaces and local paths resolve against sources the project already chose, so they do not ask again.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">Origins you deal with routinely can be listed once under <code>trustedHosts</code> in <code>blaizio.json</code>. It is an allowlist of origins, not of items: it stops the prompt, it does not review the code.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Looking before you install</h2>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Registry/TrustCommands" />
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground"><CmdChip Command="blaizio view" /> prints every file an item carries without writing anything, which is the honest way to read unfamiliar source. <code>--dry-run</code> shows the whole plan instead: which files would be written and where, which packages would be added, and which sibling items get pulled in behind the one you asked for.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">What is bounded, and what is not</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 ps-6 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
            <li><b class="text-foreground">Bounded.</b> An item writes only inside your project: component files into the configured output folder, <code>registry:file</code>/<code>registry:page</code> files at their <code>~/</code> project-root targets. Item names cannot smuggle path segments, and any path that would escape its root is rejected.</li>
            <li><b class="text-foreground">Bounded.</b> Everything an install did is recorded in <code>blaizio.json</code>, so <CmdChip Command="blaizio remove" /> undoes exactly that, by record rather than by pattern.</li>
            <li><b class="text-foreground">Not bounded.</b> The source you accepted is code in your app. So are the NuGet packages the item declared, which are ordinary dependencies with ordinary reach.</li>
            <li><b class="text-foreground">Not bounded.</b> A registry is a live URL. What it serves today is not necessarily what it served last week, and there is no version pinning yet.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">If you publish one</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">Run <CmdChip Command="blaizio registry validate" /> in CI, serve over HTTPS, keep <code>nugetDependencies</code> to what the source actually references, and describe items honestly: the description is what a consumer reads before deciding to look further. Registries listed on the <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="community">community page</a> are maintained by their authors and are not audited by Blaizio.</p>
    </section>
</div>
